    "notes": "Jurassic Finance token sale (jurassic_fi_token_sale) — a real-world-asset sale: a museum-grade Triceratops skull fractionalised as $TRCH1, live on mainnet. Eight instructions. Measured 2026-08-19: 3 build with no lookup, 5 need a value fetched first, 0 uncallable. The lookup exists because `launch` is seeded on ['launch', launch.admin, launch.launch_id] — two fields of the account being derived. A catalogue's derive_pda dead-ends here and says so honestly: to compute the account you need what is inside it. The recipe IS known — carried from the sibling that declares it derivably — so what remains is reading two named values, `admin` and `params.launch_id`, rather than an undecidable account. Gecko reads them off the chain and derives it, then hands the accounts back to Orquestra's OWN builder. WIDTH IS LOAD-BEARING: params.launch_id is a u64 (le, width 8). Read at u8 it derives a DIFFERENT, perfectly valid address — correctly formatted, resolvable, and not the sale. No schema check catches that, which is why the width is recovered rather than assumed. CORRECTED 2026-08-19: we said for a week that `contribute` takes launch_id as an argument. It does not — `contribute` declares only `requested_amount` and `min_accepted_amount`. The `params.launch_id` seed recipe was CARRIED from `initialize_launch`, which is the only instruction that declares `params`. So a caller of `contribute` must obtain launch_id elsewhere (read it off the launch account, or know it from the sale) and supply it as a seed value. Our own gate now says exactly that instead of inviting the caller to pass an argument they were never given. Known gap: dust_token_account reads as clean in the IDL but is not buildable from it — a sibling recipe supplies the missing seed."
